# Cloudflare Pages SPA fallback — every path serves index.html (200, not a
# redirect), so react-router-dom can handle routing client-side. Without this,
# a direct/deep request for any non-root path (a shared board link, a password
# reset link, refreshing on /calendar, or a first-load race before the service
# worker's own navigateFallback is registered) gets Cloudflare's static-asset
# 404 instead of the app shell — which is exactly what can strand a user on a
# blank/dead page with no way back in. This file is the edge-level guarantee;
# the workbox `navigateFallback` in vite.config.ts is the same protection at
# the service-worker layer once a visit has installed one.
#
# ORDER MATTERS — first match wins, and Cloudflare only consults this file for
# paths that don't match a real uploaded file. The /assets/* rule MUST stay
# above the catch-all: build output is content-hashed, so when a stale
# index.html (edge-cached or service-worker-cached) asks for an asset hash from
# a previous deploy, that file no longer exists. Without this rule the catch-all
# answers "200 OK" with index.html — so the browser receives HTML where it
# expected CSS/JS, refuses to apply it (correct MIME enforcement), and the whole
# app renders completely unstyled with no console error that names the cause.
# That happened in production on 2026-08-15. A hard 404 here is honest: the
# browser reports a failed asset, and the service worker / a reload can recover.
/assets/*  /404.html  404
/*         /index.html  200
